SUMMARY = {
"001": "My undergraduate capstone at Trinity College Dublin, in Prof. Cormac McGuinness's Surface, Interface Physics &amp; X-ray Spectroscopy Group. The aim was to find the band gap of metal-porphyrin chains and where their HOMO and LUMO states are located, using density functional theory in FHI-aims.</p><p>The project found that the carbon bridge between unit cells in the 1D chain can change the HOMO-LUMO gap, where the frontier states are located and whether the chain is magnetic. In zinc chains a fully fused bridge carries the HOMO along the whole chain, while a weak bridge leaves it on each unit. With iron and vanadium centres the metal's d orbitals move into the gap and shorten it, so the frontier states are on the metal. Weakly bonded chains carry unpaired electrons on their carbon backbone, while more strongly fused chains tend to pair them up. The simulations point to the weak bridge keeping each unit's orbitals separate, so each porphyrin keeps the unpaired electrons it has as a single molecule, while fusing joins them into bands along the chain.</p><p>Furthermore, it was found that an oxygen bound to the central metal can override the effect of the carbon bridge. The backbone spin disappears, leaving a single local spin on the metal-oxygen unit (S = 1 for iron, S = ½ for vanadium), and the HOMO-LUMO gap widens, from 0.34 to 0.64 eV in the fully fused iron chain.</p><p>This points towards potential for chemical sensors and molecular electronics, both of great interest with porphyrins (Paolesse et al., 2017), given the graphene-like conduction that fused porphyrin chains can display (Gao et al., 2023).",
"002": "<em>Xylella fastidiosa</em> is a bacterium that blocks a tree's water vessels. Since it was found near Gallipoli in 2013 it has killed millions of olive trees in southern Italy, and estimates say it could cost Italy €2 to 5 billion over 50 years if the dead groves are not replanted. A trip to the region, and seeing the impact on the olive groves, got me interested in modelling the disease. This project is a recreation of the agent-based model of <a href='https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-44997-4'>Fierro, Liccardo &amp; Porcelli (2019)</a>, in which spittlebugs carry the bacterium from tree to tree, rebuilt light enough to run on my old PC and be tuned over multiple runs. The aim was to reconstruct the results of the original paper and, secondly, to bring in percolation theory and look for planting spacings and felling patterns that could break up the spread.</p><p>Via stochastic simulation, it was found that outbreaks are very much all or nothing. 39 of 40 simulated outbreaks infected the whole grove, and below roughly 16% chance of infection per spittlebug visit, outbreaks die out. With trees spaced 8 m apart, and spittlebug numbers per tree kept down by managing the ground cover, the infection no longer spreads through the whole grove. None of the felling patterns tried slowed the spread.",
"003": "Lattice models (Ising and Potts models) were of big interest to me in college, as was the topic of mathematical ecology. This project is an attempt to represent a coral reef as a Potts model, a grid of cells, each pulled towards the state of its neighbours via nearest-neighbour interaction. A patch with healthy neighbours is more likely to stay healthy, and a patch with bleached neighbours is more likely to bleach. In the global bleaching event of 2023-25, about 84% of the world's reef area had heat stress high enough to bleach coral, and an earlier event at Scott Reef in Western Australia in 2016 gave me bleaching surveys to study. The best-known reef models, such as that of <a href='https://doi.org/10.1038/nature06252'>Mumby, Hastings &amp; Edwards (2007)</a>, are mean-field, where every patch of reef feels one uniform field, given by the health of the reef as a whole.</p><p>The aim was to rebuild my undergraduate Potts model in the form of a lattice reef, with patches of healthy coral, bleached coral, bare rock and seaweed, and to investigate how the reach of the nearest-neighbour interaction changes the way a reef recovers, compared with the mean-field models in standard use. From physics we know that mean-field models exaggerate tipping points, and I felt that nearest-neighbour coupling would better reflect how real reefs recover. The model was then fitted to the 2016 bleaching event at Scott Reef.</p><p>Via stochastic simulation, it was found that the reef has hysteresis (warmed and then cooled back, it returns along a lower path than it went out on) at every interaction range. Once heat kills enough coral, seaweed grows over the dead coral and blocks new coral from settling, so the coral only returns once the water is cooled well below the temperature where it was lost. In the mean-field reef the gap between the warming and cooling paths is about 60% wider than in the nearest-neighbour reef, where each patch feels only its four nearest neighbours. Cooled back to the starting temperature, the mean-field reef stays degraded at 0.49 coral cover, while the nearest-neighbour reef recovers to about 0.9.</p><p>Real corals differ in how much heat they can take, so each patch of the model reef has its own heat tolerance, drawn at random and smoothed so that neighbouring colonies are alike. The model was fitted to 65 surveys of Scott Reef from April 2016 by simulation-based inference. 8,000 simulated reefs were run through the 2015-16 heatwave and surveyed the same way as the real reef, and a neural network learned which parameters produce which survey. The fit estimates the corals' mean heat tolerance, its spread across the reef, how steeply corals respond once past their tolerance, and the noise. Two different reefs fit the surveys equally well. In the sensitive reef the corals start to feel heat stress at about 4.5 degree heating weeks (a measure of the heat built up over the last 12 weeks), and in the tolerant reef at about 12.7, but they respond more steeply once past it. The surveys were taken at 16.5 degree heating weeks, far beyond both, when both reefs had bleached about 85% of their coral, so they cannot tell the two apart. There seems to be an interplay between the stress threshold and the severity of the reaction past it, which would need further study.</p><p>This model was built for fun, and it could be improved a lot. Access to better coral bleaching data, better data assimilation, a more precise loss function for tuning the parameters and a closer match to the biological processes at play would make for a much more interesting simulation. Real reefs, for example, recover slowly from coral larvae carried in from other reefs, which this model leaves out.",
"004": "I have always found the desertification process interesting. After reading a few papers on desertification and on spread models, I found it was quite similar to previous projects I had done, and that it suited the GIS skills I have learned over the last year. This project is a recreation of the facilitation model of <a href='https://doi.org/10.1038/nature06111'>Kéfi et al. (2007)</a>, and the code is a spin-off of my coral reef lattice, in which bare soil cells recover faster next to plant cells.</p><p>The aim was to mimic the desertification dynamics seen in real satellite images and NDVI (a satellite measure of greenness). We examined the lattice's tipping points and how its behaviour correlates with rainfall, and compared it with theories that ignore space. The lattice was then driven with real rain and compared, using a loss function, against satellite greenness at two Sahel sites and then at 223 sites from Senegal to Chad.</p><p>Via stochastic simulation, it was found that with strong facilitation, recovering from desert takes much more rain than the collapse did. It was also found that the size of the vegetation patches falls before the overall cover does. The largest patch first holds 90% of the vegetation and then only 6%, while the cover is still near a half, so the patch-size distribution proved to be a good early warning of collapse, well before it happens.</p><p>Rain alone does not explain the behaviour of the real sites. Gourma's greenness tracks its rain and Fakara's does not as strongly. Given this, a study across the whole Sahel took the rainfall signal out of each site's greenness trend and looked for other factors that correlate with what is left, such as cropland, livestock, population and soil. Land recovery tracks both rainfall and land use, among other factors, so precipitation cannot be looked at in isolation. With seed rain added (each year a few seeds arrive on the lattice from outside it), the lattice was tested against satellite records from 1982-2021. Its cover correlates with the real greenness about as well as rainfall alone does, and slightly better from year to year. A tipping point would show as land that does not recover when the rain returns, and the satellite record cannot separate this from the effect of the rain itself.</p><p>A more successful simulation would include land use and its intensity over time, and would test prevention methods such as rotating grazing against simulated droughts.",
"005": "In my work in quantitative climate risk at Wieldmore, I became interested in regional climate models, smaller region-specific models of the mesoclimate. The Po basin produces about 40% of Italy's GDP. It is a manageable enough size to apply a PDE mesh and machine learning techniques to directly, and it has rich sources of data, ERA5-Land, with boxes about 9 km across, and a network of weather stations.</p><p>The aim was a calibrated 1 km model of daily maximum temperature for the Po Valley, anchored to the network of station readings. A heat-budget equation (a balance of the heat carried by the wind, mixed through the air and exchanged with the ground), anchored to the stations, was compared with a U-Net (a neural network that turns images into images), a physics-informed U-Net, ERA5-Land and standard statistical regression. The models were run on ERA5-Land, MODIS and 38 stations for the summers of 2021-2023.</p><p>Each model was scored by its root-mean-square error (RMSE) in kelvin (K, where 1 K is a 1 °C difference) at held-out stations. It was found that every regional model has about a sixth less error than ERA5-Land (about 1.60 K against 1.89 K). The regression, the heat-budget model and the physics-informed U-Net are within 0.05 K of each other. A U-Net trained on the stations alone does worse (1.71 K), and adding the heat budget to its training loss, which makes it the physics-informed U-Net, brings it level with the others. Anchored to the stations, the physics-informed U-Net is the best model on the hottest 5% of days (1.60 K against 2.05 K for ERA5-Land).</p><p>We trained and anchored the models on real station data, and 38 stations were not enough for an adequate split into training and test stations, so it is very hard to evaluate these models against each other. A denser network of stations, such as those of the regional environmental agencies (ARPA), is needed to separate them.",
"006": "A fleet of solar plants carries correlated risk. If each site's P95, its output in a bad year, is worked out on its own, the fleet's bad year is underpriced. This is because the sites are spatially correlated. The same cloud and weather events can cover many sites at once, and one site's climate is correlated with another site's climate. This project built a quick framework for measuring that correlated risk. This step has to come before simulated or stochastic weather can be used to price a portfolio's risk.</p><p>The aim was to measure the extent of correlation between 25 Spanish PV locations, and to assess its impact on the fleet's output and revenue at OMIE prices. ERA5 data define the correlated risk baseline (through correlations, copulas and similar tools), and ECMWF's physics (IFS) and AI (AIFS) forecasts are then tested as tools to monitor and project this correlated risk structure into the future.</p><p>It was found that the correlation between sites falls to 1/e (about 0.37, the usual measure of how far a decaying correlation reaches) over 350 to 670 km, depending on the season. So the 25-site portfolio behaves more like 3 to 4 independent sites, through a lack of climatic diversification. On the worst 5% of days in 2023, the fleet produced 16% less than an independent risk model would suggest. Over 30 years of ERA5, the fleet's 1-in-20 bad month is 10.5% below normal, four times deeper than the 2.5% that independent sites give, and a copula event set (simulated months that keep the correlation between sites) reproduces this. It was also found that forecast errors are far more local than the weather, and in general cancel out over a spatially spread portfolio.</p><p>The project reinforces the need to always model the correlated risk structure of a solar fleet, based on shared weather and also on a shared market. I would love to repeat the analysis on downscaled weather, including the impact of correlated market risk.",
"007": "In July 2021 a frost in Brazil's coffee belt raised the arabica price by a third in a week. Frost was meant to be the basis of this project, but frost events turned out to be very rare, so the project instead looks at the more common weather risks of the two main coffee origins: drought in Brazil, where arabica is grown, and heat and drought in Vietnam, where robusta is grown.</p><p>The aim was to measure the correlation structure between the weather at each origin and the price of each coffee bean, the impact on price of compound events (extreme weather in both countries at once), and whether ECMWF's seasonal forecast can tell a European importer when to buy forward. Prices are World Bank monthly prices for 2004-2026, with the impact of currency fluctuations (the Brazilian real) removed, and ERA5 gives the weather statistics at each origin.</p><p>Via extreme value analysis, tail dependence and copulas, it was found that heat in Vietnam is the clearest weather signal. Of the hottest 10% of months in Vietnam, 32% were also top-10% price rises for robusta, and the same share for arabica. Across all months, Vietnam heat has a rank correlation of about 0.17 with each bean once the currency is held fixed. It moves robusta in the same month and leads arabica by about five months, so a hit to robusta knocks on to arabica. In the 16 separate months when Brazil and Vietnam were both hit by extreme weather at once, both beans rose about 3.5% on average in the month. The two bean prices also spike together. When one bean has a top-10% month, the other does too about half the time, and this share has risen since 2013.</p><p>This shows the potential of forecast information for buying decisions and price hedging, for example with options, although with about 70 quarters of data the forecast-driven buying calls could not be shown to beat climatology. It also shows that hedging between Brazil and Vietnam is not always the answer, because of compound weather events and the correlation between the two bean prices, as an impact on robusta can knock on to arabica. I would like to size a global hedge for joint weather risk, rather than for the average correlation between the beans.",
"008": "The devastating storms in Chile caused extreme disruption to copper supply networks, and got me interested in how Earth observation can help measure the impact of events like these on metal supply chains early, and so on metal prices. Copper suits this well, because its supply chain is short and concentrated. Copper concentrate leaves a handful of Chilean and Peruvian ports and is smelted in only a few dozen plants, so ship traffic at the ports and heat signatures from the smelter furnaces can both be measured from space and could act as early price signals.</p><p>The aim was to build free, open versions of these two signals, ship traffic from IMF PortWatch and furnace heat from Sentinel-2's shortwave infrared, and to test them against official exports, the copper price and real disruptions.</p><p>Via out-of-sample tests, it was found that dry-bulk exports from Peru's copper ports correlate with Peru's official copper exports (0.67 in monthly levels), so ship traffic at these ports can stand in for the copper leaving them. 24 of 30 smelters show a heat signature from space, and the heat of the Chilean smelters leads exports from the Chilean ports by about three months (correlation 0.37). Neither signal could predict next month's copper price out of sample.</p><p>In the central Chile storm of July 2026, dry-bulk exports from the central ports fell to zero on the first day and recovered by about 26 July, while the northern ports kept shipping. The copper price rose about 3% over the storm and 4.6% by 4 August, so the ships showed the stop the same day and the price moved within the week. Cloud and snow hid the Caletones smelter from Sentinel-2 for 39 days, but VIIRS, a satellite sensor that detects heat sources daily, caught it still running in the middle of the storm, and Sentinel-1 radar, which sees through cloud, counted the ships waiting at the port. So other Earth-observation sources can still measure heat and shipping when optical imagery cannot.</p><p>This shows these signals can measure disruptions as they happen, rather than forecast the price in steady conditions.",
"009": "This project came out of my training at work, where I recreated some of the standard papers in the field, and I have taken it further in my own time. As is well known, gas plants set the Italian power price in most hours, so the price mostly follows Dutch TTF gas. This project aims to strip the gas signal out of the Italian power price, first by modelling power relative to gas. It is a recreation of the GARCH-MIDAS analysis (a volatility model in which a monthly variable, here extreme weather, shifts the daily volatility) of Guerzoni, Riso &amp; Zoia (2026), who found extreme weather to be a significant driver of Italian power-price volatility, here on real Italy North prices for 2018-2026.</p><p>The aim was to measure what drives the price relative to gas, what drives its volatility, and how much ECMWF's seasonal weather forecast can add.</p><p>Via GARCH-MIDAS, it was found that the monthly count of extreme-weather days is significant (p ≈ 1.5 × 10⁻⁷), but with the opposite sign to the 2026 study, so months with more extreme days have calmer prices relative to gas. This is because in heatwaves gas plants set the price in every hour, so power simply follows gas, while in mild, sunny months solar sets the price for part of the day and the price swings most. Growing solar in the Italian grid may be starting to offset the effect of cooling demand on hot days. Even knowing each month's count of extreme days in advance improves the volatility forecast by only 0.7%, measured by its forecast error.</p><p>In a covariate analysis of daily prices, it was found that demand, renewable production, heat and the day of the week are the main predictors of the power price relative to gas, as is commonly understood. Together they explain 47% of the daily swings out of sample, and in summer 2026 power ran about 11% higher relative to gas on days above 28 °C.</p><p>ECMWF's seasonal forecast added nothing over a normal season when used to predict the next three months, as the weather's signal in Italian power is day to day and a three-month average washes it out. It would be very interesting to take this further with the AI ensemble forecasts and stochastic event sets now available. Using weather to inform energy trading is already common practice, and there has never been more potential for it.",
"015": "On 31 July 2026 the Rhine at Kaub fell to 25 cm, matching its 2018 record, and a month of low water is estimated to cost about 1% of German industrial output (<a href='https://doi.org/10.1515/ger-2022-0077'>Ademmer, Jannsen &amp; Meuchelb&ouml;ck 2023</a>). This project is a maths note on how far one failure spreads through a connected network, using tools from ecology that <a href='https://doi.org/10.1038/451893a'>May, Levin &amp; Sugihara (2008)</a> proposed for banking. These are May's stability bound, percolation thresholds and disassortative wiring.</p><p>The aim was to derive these tools, check them by simulation, and apply them to German industry when the Rhine runs dry and to a structured credit portfolio.</p><p>Via a calibrated supply network of 4,000 firms, it was found that the same 1% loss is reproduced by economies anywhere from 6% to 89% of the way to their percolation threshold, and that past the threshold the same low water costs about 38% of a month's output. Furthermore, it was found that with the same failure chance for each firm, the worst 1% of months loses 1.8% of output with independent failures, 2.3% through the network and 7.4% with a standard correlation model, and that in a loan pool at equal mean loss the 15-30% tranche carries 46 times the expected loss of a Vasicek model.</p><p>This points towards measuring how easily road and rail can replace the barges, since that, rather than the river level, sets how close the network is to its threshold.",
"010": "Seismology is built on signal processing, which ties it to my other projects, and earth systems are where my passion in applied mathematics lies. Southern California has about 10,000 earthquakes a year, most of them too small to feel, and under Los Angeles many are buried in traffic and other noise.</p><p>The aim was to find which denoising methods keep what a seismologist needs from a recording, the P-wave arrival time and the first motion (whether the ground moves up or down first). Filters, wavelets and a random-matrix denoiser were scored on 3,000 of the noisiest labelled recordings from the Southern California Seismic Network (Ross, Meier &amp; Hauksson, 2018), and minimum-variance stacking on a synthetic array.</p><p>It was found that the random-matrix denoiser picked 98% of arrivals only because every recording had its arrival at the same point. With the arrivals moved at random it picked 60%, against 56% with no denoising. A plain bandpass filter was the only method that helped (63%) without changing any first motions, while wavelets added about 3 dB of signal-to-noise but flipped the first motion on about one trace in fourteen.</p><p>Furthermore, it was found on the synthetic array that minimum-variance weights cancel noise shared between neighbouring sensors (13.9 dB against 10.9 dB for a plain average of 32), but only once the noise covariance is cleaned with shrinkage, from 0.2 s of noise rather than 0.8 s.",
"011": "I built this small Python library to support my other projects, since most of them involve some kind of correlation, signal processing, filtering or data assimilation. I wanted one set of tools I knew worked, so that when I code with AI agents the tools they call are always consistent and correct, and building it made me learn the mathematics behind each method.</p><p>The aim was one library for correlation measures, estimators for messy data, factor removal, random-matrix cleaning and array stacking, each tested against a known answer or an established library. There are 59 tests, and the rank correlations and Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage match SciPy and scikit-learn.</p><p>On ten years of daily returns for 39 US stocks, it was found that removing the market raises the effective number of independent bets from about 7 to 15, and the sectors appear as blocks once the matrix is ordered by clustering. Furthermore, it was found that cleaning a one-year matrix closes only a small part of the gap between the risk a minimum-variance portfolio predicts and the risk it delivers, with realised risk 1.41 to 1.48 times the prediction for every method.</p><p>The library is the shared engine of the Spanish solar portfolio (006) and the seismic denoiser (010).",
"012": "In commodities the most important hedger is often the producer, a farm that knows roughly how much it will harvest but not what it will be paid. Russia's 2010 heatwave and grain export ban give one real test of its choices. European wheat rose 39%, from €136 a tonne on 3 May to €189 on 31 August.</p><p>The aim was to test the eight hedges producers use day to day, set up on 3 May the way an Italian soft-wheat producer would have set them up, and then see what each paid. An Italian miller on the other side of the market hedges with the mirror-image tools, and the options are priced by Black (1976).</p><p>Via Monte Carlo under the pricing model, it was found that the producer did best unhedged in hindsight (€189 a tonne against €136 with futures), but that judged on 3 May the ranking reverses, with futures and a zero-cost collar protecting best against the worst 5% of outcomes. Furthermore, it was found that the miller saved €53 a tonne by buying futures, and that for a producer whose harvest falls with the price rise the best hedge drops from 100% of the expected crop to about half.</p><p>This points towards choosing a hedge by which side of the market a firm is on and what it cannot afford. I would like to repeat the test across other shocks on Euronext prices.",
"014": "A learning project. I learned option pricing from Mark Joshi's <em>The Concepts and Practice of Mathematical Finance</em>, before and during my time as a quantitative analyst at Wieldmore, where I dealt with many kinds of options beyond European ones, Asian options among them. It tied in well with the PDEs and numerical methods from my physics degree. My main interest is commodities, and an Asian option pays on the average price over a period, which is how commodity buyers usually hedge, because they buy steadily rather than on one day.</p><p>The aim was to price and hedge an Asian option on coffee, cocoa and sugar futures with volatility that follows the calendar, building every price at least two independent ways: Monte Carlo with a control variate, moment matching, a finite-difference solver and Večeř's one-dimensional PDE.</p><p>It was found that the three methods agree, with Večeř's PDE within one Monte Carlo standard error (about 0.0003 on a price near 8), and that the geometric Asian as a control variate cuts the Monte Carlo variance by 1,100 to 7,200 times. Furthermore, the simulations reproduce well-known results. The Asian's delta fades as the average locks in, but most of its hedging risk comes just before and at the start of averaging, and ignoring seasonal volatility changes the price (by about 1% for coffee) but not how well the hedge works.</p><p>The same pricing and delta hedging carry into the wheat hedging project (012).",
"013": "Undergraduate numerical simulation work in C++: Laplace's equation solved on the unit square by successive over-relaxation (SOR), which pushes each update past the neighbour average so it converges faster. I later ported the solver to Python with Numba to study the compute cost against the relaxation factor and grid size, and applied the same SOR machinery to the Black-Scholes equation.",
"017": "Undergraduate numerical simulation work in C++. A fourth-order Runge-Kutta integrator, written by hand, solves dx/dt = (t - 2)²(x + 1) and is checked against the exact solution. Its error falls as the fourth power of the step size. A boundary-value problem is then solved by the shooting method, bisecting on the starting slope.",
"016": "Undergraduate Monte Carlo work in C++: the Potts model sampled with the Metropolis algorithm on a 24 x 24 lattice. For q = 3 the transition is continuous, near β ≈ 1.04-1.06, close to the exact value ln(1 + √3) ≈ 1.005. For q = 5 it is first order and much sharper. The same model, scaled up and given biological rules, became the coral reef project (003).",
}


def thumb(folder, name):
    """Centre-crop to 8:5 and save at 2x the 96 x 60 display size."""
    src = os.path.join(ROOT, folder, "figures", name)
    im = Image.open(src)
    im.seek(min(getattr(im, "n_frames", 1) - 1, 40))  # a frame part-way into an animation
    im = im.convert("RGB")
    w, h = im.size
    tw, th = (w, round(w * 5 / 8)) if w * 5 / 8 <= h else (round(h * 8 / 5), h)
    l, t = (w - tw) // 2, (h - th) // 2
    out = im.crop((l, t, l + tw, t + th)).resize((192, 120), Image.LANCZOS)
    key = folder.split("/")[-1]
    os.makedirs(THUMBS, exist_ok=True)
    out.save(os.path.join(THUMBS, key + ".png"), optimize=True)
    return f"images/thumbs/{key}.png"


